WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.811
Aesop’s Fables. Part 7.

00:00:02.811 --> 00:00:04.675
The Fox and the Monkey.

00:00:04.675 --> 00:00:11.912
A Fox and a Monkey were on the road together, and fell into a dispute as to which of the two was the better born.

00:00:11.912 --> 00:00:22.982
They kept it up for some time, till they came to a place where the road passed through a cemetery full of monuments, when the Monkey stopped and looked about him and gave a great sigh.

00:00:22.982 --> 00:00:25.746
"Why do you sigh?" said the Fox.

00:00:25.746 --> 00:00:35.541
The Monkey pointed to the tombs and replied, "All the monuments that you see here were put up in honour of my forefathers, who in their day were eminent men."

00:00:35.541 --> 00:00:49.480
The Fox was speechless for a moment, but quickly recovering he said, "Oh! don't stop at any lie, sir; you're quite safe: I'm sure none of your ancestors will rise up and expose you."

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.930
Boasters brag most when they cannot be detected.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:54.890
The Ass and the Lap-Dog.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:58.475
There was once a man who had an Ass and a Lap-dog.

00:00:58.475 --> 00:01:05.112
The Ass was housed in the stable with plenty of oats and hay to eat and was as well off as an ass could be.

00:01:05.112 --> 00:01:18.795
The little Dog was made a great pet of by his master, who fondled him and often let him lie in his lap; and if he went out to dinner, he would bring back a tit-bit or two to give him when he ran to meet him on his return.

00:01:18.795 --> 00:01:35.136
The Ass had, it is true, a good deal of work to do, carting or grinding the corn, or carrying the burdens of the farm: and ere long he became very jealous, contrasting his own life of labour with the ease and idleness of the Lap-dog.

00:01:35.136 --> 00:01:51.608
At last one day he broke his halter, and frisking into the house just as his master sat down to dinner, he pranced and capered about, mimicking the frolics of the little favourite, upsetting the table and smashing the crockery with his clumsy efforts.

00:01:51.608 --> 00:01:59.584
Not content with that, he even tried to jump on his master's lap, as he had so often seen the dog allowed to do.

00:01:59.584 --> 00:02:10.767
At that the servants, seeing the danger their master was in, belaboured the silly Ass with sticks and cudgels, and drove him back to his stable half dead with his beating.

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:15.511
"Alas!" he cried, "all this I have brought on myself.

00:02:15.511 --> 00:02:24.851
Why could I not be satisfied with my natural and honourable position, without wishing to imitate the ridiculous antics of that useless little Lap-dog?"

00:02:24.851 --> 00:02:26.910
The Fir-Tree and the Bramble.

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:36.130
A Fir-tree was boasting to a Bramble, and said, somewhat contemptuously, "You poor creature, you are of no use whatever.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:45.666
Now, look at me: I am useful for all sorts of things, particularly when men build houses; they can't do without me then."

00:02:45.666 --> 00:02:56.962
But the Bramble replied, "Ah, that's all very well: but you wait till they come with axes and saws to cut you down, and then you'll wish you were a Bramble and not a Fir."

00:02:56.962 --> 00:03:01.274
Better poverty without a care than wealth with its many obligations.

00:03:01.274 --> 00:03:03.853
The Frogs' Complaint Against the Sun.

00:03:03.853 --> 00:03:08.223
Once upon a time the Sun was about to take to himself a wife.

00:03:08.223 --> 00:03:17.568
The Frogs in terror all raised their voices to the skies, and Jupiter, disturbed by the noise, asked them what they were croaking about.

00:03:17.568 --> 00:03:25.154
They replied, "The Sun is bad enough even while he is single, drying up our marshes with his heat as he does.

00:03:25.154 --> 00:03:29.485
But what will become of us if he marries and begets other Suns?"

00:03:29.485 --> 00:03:32.499
The Dog, the Cock, and the Fox.

00:03:33.114 --> 00:03:38.251
A Dog and a Cock became great friends, and agreed to travel together.

00:03:38.251 --> 00:03:47.362
At nightfall the Cock flew up into the branches of a tree to roost, while the Dog curled himself up inside the trunk, which was hollow.

00:03:47.362 --> 00:03:51.789
At break of day the Cock woke up and crew, as usual.

00:03:51.789 --> 00:03:59.513
A Fox heard, and, wishing to make a breakfast of him, came and stood under the tree and begged him to come down.

00:03:59.513 --> 00:04:05.727
"I should so like," said he, "to make the acquaintance of one who has such a beautiful voice."

00:04:05.727 --> 00:04:11.020
The Cock replied, "Would you just wake my porter who sleeps at the foot of the tree?

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:13.481
He'll open the door and let you in."

00:04:13.481 --> 00:04:19.733
The Fox accordingly rapped on the trunk, when out rushed the Dog and tore him in pieces.
